.TH SYS_WANT 2
.SH NAME
sys_want, sys_wanted \- servers started on demand, for the supervisor (SYS_WANT, SYS_WANTED)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_want(const char *name, long error);
long sys_wanted(char *buf, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The supervisor,
.BR init (8),
starts some servers only when needed: the network, the assistant...
.PP
.B sys_want
with
.I error
0 declares
.I name
(a port name, at most 15 characters) as such a server's: from then on, a
.BR port_lookup (2)
that finds no such port tells the supervisor (a "token 0" message with
.B NOTE_WANT
on its child port,
.BR sys_child_port (2))
instead of waiting for nobody. Up to 16 names. With a negative
.IR error ,
the threads waiting for
.I name
stop waiting and get that error: the server cannot start.
.PP
.B sys_wanted
copies into
.I buf
the declared names that some thread waits for right now and that have no
port yet, each ended by a 0 byte ("net\e0sia\e0"); the supervisor starts
their servers, and the waiters wake when the port appears, as with any
server that starts late. No polling anywhere. What does not fit in
.I size
is left out.
.PP
Only the supervisor may call them.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sys_want :
0, or a negative error.
.BR sys_wanted :
the number of bytes copied, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not the supervisor.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.RB ( sys_want )
An empty or too long name, or an
.I error
above 0 or below -4095.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.RB ( sys_want )
16 names are declared already.
.TP
.B EFAULT
A pointer is not the caller's memory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR port_lookup (2),
.BR sys_child_port (2),
.BR init (8),
.BR svc (8)
